{:edb/db-before-t 1314 :edb/db-after-t 1315 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534627 50 #inst "2026-10-12T05:08:58.953Z" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221169 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=-5,$ with corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [3, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-2, \\ 1]^T,$ respectively. Using this information, sketch the phase portrait of the system." 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221169 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=-5,$ with corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [3, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-2, \\ 1]^T,$ respectively. Using this information, sketch the phase portrait of the system." 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186221169 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n![](images/b6/b6e1ba9b197e105bfa8599f81ef6a15381cf2d996f75528733b8bf5f32ed1f66.svg)\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ![](images/c8/c83c3bdb26e609cf9e5d023ea9e4f4bf366162dc9957972355b0eaab06c42a44.svg) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c39f92243806a8682701374244fa2f7def32ab56ae509c920a31e75abfacb88f.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/bf/bf953962efa5f9c6f59878328bd99be9f795d31e05069e8925b520a48eedc3c7.svg) has the equation \n$y=- \\dfrac12x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/0e/0eec91967012c45aadd7ed416606e7ff0f5d9fdbfe7a9a3b75a24108a6160d00.svg)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{-5t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/96/96a198c7fe3d98d3775ffc176b6659022b18676caf849cbc8d5c66833113bb19.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/74/74b573da705aafba25c74aa789dc3a51464683dd5a47a999ba9c557d35ed1d02.png)" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221169 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n![](images/cf/cfff34f683e4c790f0f669a1babde2e9da9fcc10769c674f037ed3328d658f9f.png)\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ![](images/75/75c8006b52acb68b62c4f827fdd7b8c08709c647c7994f3088bf4f8f72eb3b8f.png) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c39f92243806a8682701374244fa2f7def32ab56ae509c920a31e75abfacb88f.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/23/236a2688de746c23166d9159dfc073781ed55bc33cb289230f8bc9dbc00e323d.png) has the equation \n$y=- \\dfrac12x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/25/2527c2ffbb46a109eac9386b6b4bb634ca98768cf261252a2b94daf72f11a4a0.png)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{-5t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/96/96a198c7fe3d98d3775ffc176b6659022b18676caf849cbc8d5c66833113bb19.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/74/74b573da705aafba25c74aa789dc3a51464683dd5a47a999ba9c557d35ed1d02.png)" 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186221170 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$  Sketch the phase portrait of the system." 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221170 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$  Sketch the phase portrait of the system." 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186221170 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/32/32cdfec727ba37d7c21b4df53d40d29853c56a0bf7c5469227882de8bceb735a.png) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/81/810d48ff45c63e14ede1c9b7742298b263c861bba689595af6fe7afeac69f96d.png) are equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfc7b00f7f63aec120179da3f89f72f0aa53f13d299c95ed8f53e6238d329e51.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n![](images/93/93ec8854dd09842895e8f0854683a03c2cae129198802f9b49c3d426179eb78b.png)\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t+2 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/55/55dfdfce480deba74fe46c1634e4c7ffb711fbd617525cdb5387bdabfc83ae75.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t-2 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/2f/2fb2297c741ea20ca729dcacb9934d7f8d1cca58af93c2b75db177890934b2b6.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e7970ba2d6bbe4745ee498cdbc72f2b7d8689219291847644bd6483696cc456.png)" 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186221170 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/b0/b0b5abdfe48d45a352b101e134ebb06165824385c1162a705e539f911af9f267.svg) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/96/962887c2e859c2afadfc122bd5ecc22fe28eb354b348c5153c16577de5fe37d8.svg) are equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfc7b00f7f63aec120179da3f89f72f0aa53f13d299c95ed8f53e6238d329e51.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n![](images/9b/9bbb4470407c8498cf3270e247e7b9a9d214bd1ff1b3d85a75d17189bee26ea9.svg)\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t+2 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/55/55dfdfce480deba74fe46c1634e4c7ffb711fbd617525cdb5387bdabfc83ae75.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t-2 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/2f/2fb2297c741ea20ca729dcacb9934d7f8d1cca58af93c2b75db177890934b2b6.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e7970ba2d6bbe4745ee498cdbc72f2b7d8689219291847644bd6483696cc456.png)" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221171 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above." 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221171 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above." 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186221171 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/1e/1e5a8eef63597c74566c624df6f52cf446c13d6d0ae16612b5ac3c45479f8816.png)\nwhere ![](images/91/9169c7aaf58e872a751777fffb26d435573ece6d79b1af64ccbe4b1bf6544463.png)\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+2y=0 \\\\ 5x+5y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+2y=0\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n2y=-2x\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n![](images/72/72ee846ffe127bc7d4fb9e7b37b633f2c07e71f439c18471792e05fb56d0f7e1.png)\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-x.$" 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186221171 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/9d/9d8df402a06960cd5f2e0c772a5fab345e4610cc90c7e88202dab34f2ab97e65.svg)\nwhere ![](images/58/58ab8470c661bcc5f0c95df2640185f95fc9733c1f4bf718ce51dba7a0b9a989.svg)\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+2y=0 \\\\ 5x+5y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+2y=0\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n2y=-2x\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n![](images/b9/b9059ea5506ed372cb5b21771b177966be2c607934164b2ad16466a31d423834.svg)\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-x.$" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221405 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429959 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429960 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221471 1050 960.0 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221951 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186221951 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186222300 1061 588356 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186222300 1061 836100 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186222301 1061 588357 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186222301 1061 836101 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186222302 1061 588360 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186222302 1061 836104 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186222303 1061 588361 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186222303 1061 836105 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186222304 1061 588364 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186222304 1061 836108 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186222305 1061 588365 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186222305 1061 836109 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186222484 1064 "In this topic, we'll study phase portraits of linear systems\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhen the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at least one *zero eigenvalue*.\n\nA zero eigenvalue is special because $e^{0\\cdot t}=1,$ so the corresponding component of a solution has *no exponential growth or decay*. Geometrically, this often creates a **line of equilibrium points** (infinitely many fixed points), instead of a single equilibrium at the origin.\n\nIndeed, equilibrium points satisfy $\\mathbf{x}'=\\mathbf{0},$ so for $\\mathbf{x}'=A\\mathbf{x}$ we solve\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThat is, equilibrium points form the *null space* of $A.$\n\n- If $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the null space is $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ and the only equilibrium is the origin.\n\n- If $\\det(A)=0,$ then the null space contains infinitely many points (typically a line through the origin in $2\\times2$ cases).\n\nA zero eigenvalue guarantees $\\det(A)=0$, so in that case the null space is nontrivial.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186222484 1064 "In this topic, we'll study phase portraits of linear systems\n![](images/6f/6f6a92e2b8fb89f833268a5526876cb3af5215db0193a2aaaad8aac5790752ea.svg)\nwhen the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at least one *zero eigenvalue*.\n\nA zero eigenvalue is special because $e^{0\\cdot t}=1,$ so the corresponding component of a solution has *no exponential growth or decay*. Geometrically, this often creates a **line of equilibrium points** (infinitely many fixed points), instead of a single equilibrium at the origin.\n\nIndeed, equilibrium points satisfy $\\mathbf{x}'=\\mathbf{0},$ so for $\\mathbf{x}'=A\\mathbf{x}$ we solve\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThat is, equilibrium points form the *null space* of $A.$\n\n- If $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the null space is $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ and the only equilibrium is the origin.\n\n- If $\\det(A)=0,$ then the null space contains infinitely many points (typically a line through the origin in $2\\times2$ cases).\n\nA zero eigenvalue guarantees $\\det(A)=0$, so in that case the null space is nontrivial.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186222488 1064 "Suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2\\neq 0$ with eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nThen, the eigenvector direction for $\\lambda_1=0$ gives a **line of equilibria**:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_E = c_1 \\mathbf{v}_1, \\qquad c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nThe other eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$ determines what happens off the equilibrium line:\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ trajectories move *away* from the equilibrium line (*unstable* direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ trajectories move *toward* the equilibrium line (*stable* direction).\n\nA typical solution has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2,\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSo trajectories are *parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2,$* shifted by the equilibrium vector $c_1\\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we know the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=3,$ with corresponding eigenvectors ![](images/47/47c0d53d1eed7289cee7d61cfaf1e8a62bccc084f445b5ca217c9b3bec78b0d8.svg) and ![](images/99/998a77eb9b491e2d1e733a0dfe1a65cc1d39759650e36c671ca1c2aa1064e29a.svg) respectively. \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system. \n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/e7/e71f95804ab65c6d60a907f33967a744b13e2c977ce4d518740a8470cc3a4e06.svg)\n\n- First, notice that ![](images/71/715228dc0a53bd16e4f6b055c9a4bc4679d8761e7a461e61f58003ffa8563e42.svg) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/4186349824bab0f592f8e951f2b0ae7472e7879c693b0271af9e824bbc67f112.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/47/4734b2299d6f70fec85b91c2d6e74332ed722dc7522d497ef8bb4e80682d3052.svg) has the equation \n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/4f/4f83cfd4f18ee670366bc8987a4c87b900ab9f5226fec8d18440627a0db6c6d0.svg)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{3t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/95/95c8829e56ce60e3fd877757c0063878e283f65a2da327bbd64ae80d725fbd81.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7bb6a132d08721ef0d96e6e47dc3089de551e9551524f47de0af495a0b9418f5.png)" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186222488 1064 "Suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2\\neq 0$ with eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nThen, the eigenvector direction for $\\lambda_1=0$ gives a **line of equilibria**:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_E = c_1 \\mathbf{v}_1, \\qquad c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nThe other eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$ determines what happens off the equilibrium line:\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ trajectories move *away* from the equilibrium line (*unstable* direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ trajectories move *toward* the equilibrium line (*stable* direction).\n\nA typical solution has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2,\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSo trajectories are *parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2,$* shifted by the equilibrium vector $c_1\\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we know the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=3,$ with corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ respectively. \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system. \n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- First, notice that $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/4186349824bab0f592f8e951f2b0ae7472e7879c693b0271af9e824bbc67f112.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since $e^{3t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/95/95c8829e56ce60e3fd877757c0063878e283f65a2da327bbd64ae80d725fbd81.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7bb6a132d08721ef0d96e6e47dc3089de551e9551524f47de0af495a0b9418f5.png)" 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186308503 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186308503 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186308503 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(2x+y)=4x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186308503 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(2x+y)=4x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" 13194139534627 17592186045417 false] [17592186308515 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186308515 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\tex