rue] [17592186429958 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429952 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429958 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429955 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429958 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429958 1077 "selection" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429958 1078 #edb/ref 17592186429955 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429958 1079 #uuid "f226cae3-dc9e-52a4-84a0-4560715ab839" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186429958 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429959 1069 "q-333779" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429959 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429959 1072 #uuid "b59bb2fc-6ce1-5b76-9a55-8b95129f4121" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429959 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-6y=0 \\\\ x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first equation is a multiple of the second one:\n\n$$\n3(x-2y)=3x-6y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the first equation.\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2y=0\\,⇒\\,x=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186429957 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429960 1069 "q-555982" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429960 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429960 1072 #uuid "d4195ef0-8343-52e8-8ea2-ad3314d46f09" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true] [17592186429960 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5afce1bca483dbd9bd462810c351d710469b744813cd9f54ea3234f0c7343d35.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+3 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/40/4019f96cd3c03ec9b0349a0a896951bfa4b0b8f0f828442e002de28d7c8ceb92.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-3 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/df/df4f8378d0c02f982299a349e76c204fd60fb5e014b72e01545cc5dec358aa04.png)\n\n \n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/18/1871e29694b88593a1aeb9ac87536059361f30a2e39966a9ca1180067213d15c.png)" 13194139534627 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-02d3660e-11f9-54c8-85b6-6ec7ef18c7ac" 17592186429947 "answer-0ceddc0a-1385-5226-bad7-64fd528d2bba" 17592186429948 "answer-184fcd59-a5a0-5e5a-8c3e-e81c57720e38" 17592186429949 "answer-2281703b-64cd-5882-ac83-002647f8b3f5" 17592186429950 "answer-291a6bd3-4c82-5f51-b5c7-611a2a9a4f2a" 17592186429951 "answer-7f2e036f-6092-5f13-88cc-76a83386c5e4" 17592186429952 "answer-bd1d4b62-16c5-5abd-aa19-2354fa42b4fe" 17592186429953 "answer-e7a42501-e309-59ab-8e88-d5e25b44bfbe" 17592186429954 "answer-e832ba8e-38d6-539c-9e5d-09409711b42c" 17592186429955 "answer-f92ae014-605b-50b1-89d8-1b6f8a824d92" 17592186429956 "answer-field-8f32fcaf-528f-5f58-8192-6bdabdbae6cd" 17592186429957 "answer-field-f226cae3-dc9e-52a4-84a0-4560715ab839" 17592186429958 "question-b59bb2fc-6ce1-5b76-9a55-8b95129f4121" 17592186429959 "question-d4195ef0-8343-52e8-8ea2-ad3314d46f09" 17592186429960} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
